WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 3.3.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:04.250
Liquidations and cascades.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.144
By the end, you'll know exactly what a liquidation

00:00:07.144 --> 00:00:09.755
costs you, and how to avoid ever reaching one.

00:00:11.055 --> 00:00:12.345
Here's the plan.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:15.191
Exactly what a liquidator does, mechanically, the

00:00:15.191 --> 00:00:17.995
moment your health factor crosses below one.

00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:20.349
How a single liquidation can actually

00:00:20.349 --> 00:00:22.260
trigger a cascade of more.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.196
A full worked example, continuing directly from

00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:27.820
Lesson three point two's own position.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.100
And the written defence ladder that keeps

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.945
you from ever needing to find out

00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:33.682
what any of this actually costs.

00:00:34.982 --> 00:00:36.707
What a liquidator actually does.

00:00:38.007 --> 00:00:40.340
Here's exactly what happens, the instant

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.632
your health factor drops below one.

00:00:42.632 --> 00:00:46.077
A liquidator, almost always a bot watching for exactly

00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:48.583
this, repays part of your debt, up to a

00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:52.077
limit called the close factor, often fifty percent.

00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:55.092
In exchange, it seizes collateral worth that

00:00:55.092 --> 00:00:57.638
same repaid amount, plus a bonus, the

00:00:57.638 --> 00:01:01.087
liquidation penalty, often five to ten percent.

00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:03.345
That bonus is the liquidator's profit, and

00:01:03.345 --> 00:01:05.236
it comes directly out of what would

00:01:05.236 --> 00:01:07.127
otherwise have been your collateral.

00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:11.128
Here's that same sequence, broken into its

00:01:11.128 --> 00:01:14.142
four actual steps, since “liquidator” can sound

00:01:14.142 --> 00:01:16.766
abstract until you see the mechanics.

00:01:16.766 --> 00:01:19.081
Your health factor crosses below one.

00:01:19.081 --> 00:01:21.949
A bot notices, essentially instantly; this

00:01:21.949 --> 00:01:24.650
is automated, competitive, and fast.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.674
It repays up to the

00:01:25.674 --> 00:01:27.305
close factor of your debt.

00:01:27.305 --> 00:01:30.798
And it seizes collateral worth that repayment, plus the

00:01:30.798 --> 00:01:34.229
bonus, leaving you with less collateral and less debt,

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:36.475
but a real loss on the bonus itself.

00:01:37.775 --> 00:01:39.314
One real number, so this

00:01:39.314 --> 00:01:41.030
isn't a rare edge case.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.590
Blockchain analytics have tracked billions of dollars

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:48.149
in total liquidations across major lending protocols,

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:51.020
cumulatively, since DeFi lending began.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.776
This is outside research, not this program's

00:01:53.776 --> 00:01:55.981
own data, but it's exactly why this

00:01:55.981 --> 00:01:58.799
lesson's defence ladder is worth writing down

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:01.065
now, not after your first close call.

00:02:02.365 --> 00:02:03.455
Quick check.

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.888
What does a liquidator actually receive, in

00:02:05.888 --> 00:02:08.100
exchange for repaying part of your debt?

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.879
The answer: collateral equal to the debt it repaid,

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:19.302
plus a bonus, the liquidation penalty, often five to

00:02:19.302 --> 00:02:21.950
ten percent, which is a real loss to you.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.035
Cascades.

00:02:25.335 --> 00:02:28.631
Here's exactly how a cascade actually forms, step

00:02:28.631 --> 00:02:31.597
by step, since it's the mechanism that turns

00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:34.600
an isolated event into a market-wide one.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.009
A liquidation sells seized collateral,

00:02:37.009 --> 00:02:39.410
immediately, into the open market.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.365
That sale itself pushes the price down further,

00:02:42.365 --> 00:02:45.620
the same price-impact mechanism from Module two.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.238
That further price drop pushes other, separate

00:02:48.238 --> 00:02:51.045
positions below their own health-factor line.

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:53.289
And those get liquidated too, selling

00:02:53.289 --> 00:02:56.065
more collateral, into an already-falling price.

00:02:57.365 --> 00:03:00.306
Worth being precise about why this matters even

00:03:00.306 --> 00:03:03.180
if your own position feels perfectly safe.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.165
A cascade isn't really about any single borrower's

00:03:06.165 --> 00:03:09.502
mistake; it's a mechanical feedback loop, forced selling

00:03:09.502 --> 00:03:12.136
causing more forced selling, that can move a

00:03:12.136 --> 00:03:14.660
market meaningfully in a short window.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.253
It's exactly why a comfortable buffer matters most during

00:03:18.253 --> 00:03:21.660
genuinely volatile periods, not just on an average day.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.561
Worth naming, plainly, that cascades aren't

00:03:25.561 --> 00:03:28.225
a theoretical risk this lesson invented.

00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:32.253
DeFi lending markets have genuinely experienced sharp, fast

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:36.550
periods of concentrated liquidations during major market drops,

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.974
with total liquidation volume spiking well above a

00:03:39.974 --> 00:03:43.027
typical day's level in a matter of hours.

00:03:43.027 --> 00:03:46.551
This is outside research, illustrative of the pattern,

00:03:46.551 --> 00:03:49.241
not a specific claim about any one event,

00:03:49.241 --> 00:03:51.932
but the pattern itself is well documented.

00:03:53.231 --> 00:03:54.321
Quick check.

00:03:54.321 --> 00:03:56.832
What's the actual mechanism that turns one

00:03:56.832 --> 00:03:58.876
liquidation into a cascade of more?

00:04:03.296 --> 00:04:07.259
The answer: liquidations sell collateral into the market, pushing

00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:10.922
the price down, which pushes other, separate positions below

00:04:10.922 --> 00:04:14.645
their own health-factor line, triggering further liquidations.

00:04:15.946 --> 00:04:16.951
Worked example.

00:04:18.251 --> 00:04:20.887
Here's the exact position this worked

00:04:20.887 --> 00:04:23.546
example continues from Lesson 3.2.

00:04:23.546 --> 00:04:27.795
10 ETH, $30,000 of collateral, $12,000 of

00:04:27.795 --> 00:04:30.826
debt, a health factor of 2.

00:04:30.826 --> 00:04:34.511
Now ETH falls to $1,490, just

00:04:34.511 --> 00:04:39.111
under the $1,500 liquidation price.

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:41.346
Health factor is now just under 1.

00:04:42.646 --> 00:04:45.355
Now watch exactly what the liquidator does,

00:04:45.355 --> 00:04:47.871
in this specific case, step by step.

00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:50.521
It repays six thousand dollars, fifty percent

00:04:50.521 --> 00:04:53.931
of your twelve-thousand-dollar debt, the close factor.

00:04:53.931 --> 00:04:57.378
It seizes collateral worth six thousand dollars, times

00:04:57.378 --> 00:05:00.574
one point zero five, the five-percent bonus, which

00:05:00.574 --> 00:05:03.255
is six thousand three hundred dollars.

00:05:03.255 --> 00:05:05.988
At one thousand four hundred ninety dollars

00:05:05.988 --> 00:05:08.597
per E.T.H., that's roughly four point two

00:05:08.597 --> 00:05:11.330
two eight E.T.H., seized from your position.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.137
Worth being precise about what that three

00:05:15.137 --> 00:05:18.300
hundred dollars actually represents, since it's easy

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.510
to gloss past as a small number.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.880
It's not a fee for a service you needed.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.915
It's value that leaves your position specifically

00:05:25.915 --> 00:05:28.161
because you didn't act first, on top

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:31.317
of having your collateral sold, involuntarily, near

00:05:31.317 --> 00:05:33.620
the low of that exact price move.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:35.133
The bonus is the entire

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:38.160
reason self-deleveraging beats being liquidated.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.999
Worth naming what's actually left of your

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:45.263
position after this specific liquidation, since fifty

00:05:45.263 --> 00:05:47.800
percent repaid isn't the whole story.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.377
Your debt drops from twelve

00:05:49.377 --> 00:05:51.535
thousand to six thousand dollars.

00:05:51.535 --> 00:05:54.375
Your collateral drops by that roughly four

00:05:54.375 --> 00:05:56.920
point two two eight E.T.H. seized.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.770
If price keeps falling from here, health factor

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.205
can cross below one again, and the exact

00:06:02.205 --> 00:06:04.580
same process can repeat, on what's left.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:06.970
Quick check.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.787
Why is choosing to sell some of

00:06:08.787 --> 00:06:12.024
your own collateral yourself, before liquidation, better

00:06:12.024 --> 00:06:14.125
than letting it happen automatically?

00:06:18.545 --> 00:06:21.982
The answer: you avoid the liquidation penalty entirely,

00:06:21.982 --> 00:06:24.743
and you choose the size and timing yourself,

00:06:24.743 --> 00:06:27.014
instead of a bot forcing both on you.

00:06:28.314 --> 00:06:29.399
The defence ladder.

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:33.726
Here's the actual ladder, with exact health-factor

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:36.219
levels, written down before you ever need

00:06:36.219 --> 00:06:38.359
it, not decided under pressure.

00:06:38.359 --> 00:06:40.419
At health factor two point zero:

00:06:40.419 --> 00:06:42.779
an alert fires, nothing else yet.

00:06:42.779 --> 00:06:45.255
At one point seven: repay from a reserve

00:06:45.255 --> 00:06:48.514
you've already set aside, or add more collateral.

00:06:48.514 --> 00:06:50.704
At one point five: sell part of your

00:06:50.704 --> 00:06:54.142
collateral yourself, deliberately, on your own terms.

00:06:54.142 --> 00:06:56.932
And never, under any circumstance, wait until

00:06:56.932 --> 00:06:58.752
one point one to start acting.

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:03.818
Here's what actually setting up that health-factor-two alert looks

00:07:03.818 --> 00:07:07.097
like, in practice, from the ladder's very first rung.

00:07:07.097 --> 00:07:11.252
Open your protocol's own notification settings, or a third-party

00:07:11.252 --> 00:07:15.387
monitoring tool, covered fully in Module fourteen point one.

00:07:15.387 --> 00:07:18.522
Set a trigger at your own chosen health-factor level.

00:07:18.522 --> 00:07:20.645
Point it at a channel you'll actually see

00:07:20.645 --> 00:07:22.917
quickly, not one you check once a week.

00:07:22.917 --> 00:07:25.350
And test it once, deliberately, so you know

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.562
it actually works before you need it to.

00:07:28.862 --> 00:07:32.846
Worth being precise about what “repay from reserve”

00:07:32.846 --> 00:07:36.517
at HF 1.7 actually buys you, versus waiting.

00:07:36.517 --> 00:07:40.127
Repaying debt directly raises health factor immediately, by

00:07:40.127 --> 00:07:42.895
the same formula from Lesson three point two,

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:45.602
without touching your collateral at all.

00:07:45.602 --> 00:07:48.003
Adding collateral instead raises it too,

00:07:48.003 --> 00:07:49.767
but ties up more capital.

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:52.077
Either move, taken at 1.7, costs

00:07:52.077 --> 00:07:54.757
nothing beyond the capital itself.

00:07:54.757 --> 00:07:56.875
The same move, forced at 1.1,

00:07:56.875 --> 00:07:59.487
costs the liquidation penalty on top.

00:08:00.787 --> 00:08:02.885
Put the two paths side by side, at

00:08:02.885 --> 00:08:05.763
the exact same starting point, since the entire

00:08:05.763 --> 00:08:08.042
lesson comes down to this choice.

00:08:08.042 --> 00:08:10.899
Wait, and a bot decides the size, the timing,

00:08:10.899 --> 00:08:13.632
and takes a bonus on top, at whatever price

00:08:13.632 --> 00:08:16.417
the market happens to be at that moment.

00:08:16.417 --> 00:08:18.764
Act at one point five, and you choose

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:21.234
the size, the timing, and keep the full

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:23.952
value of what you sell, no penalty attached.

00:08:25.252 --> 00:08:26.712
Here's your checklist.

00:08:26.712 --> 00:08:28.857
Do it now, before you ever need it.

00:08:28.857 --> 00:08:31.094
Your defence ladder is written, with

00:08:31.094 --> 00:08:34.357
exact health-factor levels, not vague intentions.

00:08:34.357 --> 00:08:36.808
A reserve is actually set aside to repay

00:08:36.808 --> 00:08:39.977
from, covered fully in Module twelve point four.

00:08:39.977 --> 00:08:43.092
And you know your collateral's specific price oracle,

00:08:43.092 --> 00:08:45.918
covered in Module five point three, since that's

00:08:45.918 --> 00:08:48.687
what actually decides when liquidation triggers.

00:08:49.987 --> 00:08:52.626
One last honest connection before the recap,

00:08:52.626 --> 00:08:54.621
since this ladder is only as good

00:08:54.621 --> 00:08:56.387
as the reserve behind it.

00:08:56.387 --> 00:08:58.533
Rung two of the ladder, repay from

00:08:58.533 --> 00:09:01.967
reserve, assumes that reserve genuinely exists, already

00:09:01.967 --> 00:09:05.027
funded, before health factor ever gets there.

00:09:05.027 --> 00:09:07.616
A defence ladder with an empty reserve rung

00:09:07.616 --> 00:09:10.029
is really just a two-step ladder, with a

00:09:10.029 --> 00:09:12.088
gap exactly where you need it most.

00:09:13.389 --> 00:09:14.634
Let's recap.

00:09:14.634 --> 00:09:17.422
A liquidator repays part of your debt, up to

00:09:17.422 --> 00:09:20.829
the close factor, and seizes that value in collateral,

00:09:20.829 --> 00:09:23.174
plus a bonus, a real loss to you.

00:09:23.174 --> 00:09:26.219
Cascades form because liquidations sell into the

00:09:26.219 --> 00:09:30.124
market, pushing prices down, triggering more liquidations.

00:09:30.124 --> 00:09:33.224
In the worked example, a fall to fourteen ninety

00:09:33.224 --> 00:09:36.387
cost three hundred dollars in bonus alone, on top

00:09:36.387 --> 00:09:38.964
of an involuntary sale near the low.

00:09:38.964 --> 00:09:41.658
And the defence ladder, written with exact HF

00:09:41.658 --> 00:09:44.235
levels, is what keeps you from ever finding

00:09:44.235 --> 00:09:45.934
any of that out the hard way.

00:09:47.234 --> 00:09:50.325
Write your defence ladder now, with exact health-factor

00:09:50.325 --> 00:09:53.219
levels, before you ever need it under pressure.

00:09:53.219 --> 00:09:57.209
Next up, Lesson 3.4: Borrowing strategies and looping.
